**`ArchivePak` has no constructor and `read_pak` does not validate `pak_version`**

- **ID:** `archive/design-archivepak-no-version-check`
- **Severity:** high
- **Area:** storage · **Type:** refactor
- **Location:** `src/archive/pak.rs:58-67, src/archive/types.rs:12-19`
- **Auto-fixable:** no

## Description
Current state: `ArchivePak` is a plain pub struct with all fields public. `read_pak` at pak.rs:58-67 deserializes any JSON without checking `pak_version`. A `.pak` file written by a future version with a different layout will silently deserialize as v1 with wrong data rather than returning an informative error. Additionally, callers can construct arbitrary `ArchivePak` values (including nonsensical `pak_version: 0`) without going through a validated path. Target state: (1) add version validation in `read_pak`: `if pak.pak_version != CURRENT_PAK_VERSION { return Err(MemoryError::Archive(format!("unsupported pak version: {}", pak.pak_version))); }`. (2) Consider sealing direct field construction behind a `ArchivePak::new(...)` constructor or making `pak_version` / `engine_schema_version` / `embed_dim` non-`pub`, exposed only via getters. Refactoring pattern: Validate on Deserialize + Constructor Encapsulation. Motivation: misuse-resistance — prevents silent data corruption on format evolution. Estimated scope: src/archive/types.rs, src/archive/pak.rs (2 files); also the 2 test helpers that construct `ArchivePak` directly. Prerequisites: finding #design-pak-version-magic-literal (the constant must exist first). Risk: medium — sealing fields is a public API break if the struct was re-exported and used externally; the struct is behind `pub(crate) mod archive` (lib.rs:36) so the API break is limited to crate-internal users.

## Suggested approach
// In read_pak, after deserialization:
if pak.pak_version != CURRENT_PAK_VERSION {
    return Err(MemoryError::Archive(format!(
        "unsupported pak version {}, expected {}",
        pak.pak_version, CURRENT_PAK_VERSION
    )));
}

