## Description
`index.insert(vec, &mut searcher)` at line 333 mutates the HNSW graph before the sequential-ID invariant is checked at line 334. If the assert fires: (1) the vector is already in the graph; (2) `index_to_fact` has NOT been pushed; (3) `fact_to_hnsw` has NOT been updated. `parking_lot::RwLock` does not poison on panic — the write lock is released, and subsequent lookups via `inner.index_to_fact[neighbor.index]` will index out-of-bounds for the orphaned entry, causing a subsequent panic or silent wrong answer. `build_from_db` handles the same invariant via `MemoryError::Internal` (line 119) — `notify_insert` must be consistent. Since `VectorSearchStrategy::notify_insert` has no return value, the fix requires changing the trait signature or storing a poisoned flag.

## Suggested approach
Change `notify_insert` to return `Result<(), MemoryError>` and propagate the error upward instead of panicking:
```rust
fn notify_insert(&self, fact_id: i64, embedding: &[f32]) -> Result<()> {
    let mut inner = self.inner.write();
    if let Some(&old_hnsw_id) = inner.fact_to_hnsw.get(&fact_id) {
        inner.tombstones.insert(old_hnsw_id);
    }
    let expected_id = inner.index_to_fact.len();
    let mut searcher: Searcher<u32> = Searcher::default();
    let hnsw_id = inner.index.insert(embedding.to_vec(), &mut searcher);
    if hnsw_id != expected_id {
        return Err(MemoryError::Internal(format!(
            "HNSW sequential ID invariant violated: got {hnsw_id}, expected {expected_id}"
        )));
    }
    inner.index_to_fact.push(fact_id);
    inner.fact_to_hnsw.insert(fact_id, hnsw_id);
    Ok(())
}
```
