## Description
MemoryGraph::from_snapshot iterates snap.edges (a Vec<GraphEdgeSnapshot> with no length cap, deserialized from the .snapshot sidecar via rmp_serde in engine/snapshot.rs:277) and calls add_edge for each, allocating petgraph nodes and edges plus HashMap entries. There is no bound on the number of edges processed. The snapshot is a local-filesystem sidecar that is (a) integrity-checked with blake3 (engine/snapshot.rs:270) and (b) gated by a DB fingerprint that requires active_edge_count/max_edge_id to match the live DB (engine/snapshot.rs:333). Note blake3 here is an unkeyed hash: it defends against accidental corruption, not against a local actor who can replace the sidecar (such an actor also controls the adjacent DB file). The fingerprint constraint meaningfully bounds the allocation to roughly the real graph size, which is why this is medium and not high. Residual risk: an attacker who can also rewrite the DB to inflate the edge count (so the fingerprint still matches an oversized snapshot) can drive large allocation at cold-start (CWE-400/770), and the raw rmp_serde::from_slice of payload_bytes has no element-count limit (CWE-502 unbounded-collection DoS). Severity would rise to high if snapshots ever became network- or multi-tenant-sourced.

## Suggested approach
Add a defensive cap before/within from_snapshot, derived from a configurable maximum (or the fingerprint's active_edge_count which is already validated). Example:

pub(crate) fn from_snapshot(snap: &GraphSnapshot) -> Self {
    const MAX_SNAPSHOT_EDGES: usize = 50_000_000; // or plumb a config limit
    let mut graph = Self::new();
    graph.graph = DiGraph::with_capacity(0, snap.edges.len().min(MAX_SNAPSHOT_EDGES));
    for edge in snap.edges.iter().take(MAX_SNAPSHOT_EDGES) {
        graph.add_edge(edge.source, edge.target, EdgeData {
            edge_id: edge.edge_id,
            relation_type: edge.relation_type.clone(),
            weight: edge.weight,
        });
    }
    graph
}

Better: cross-check snap.edges.len() against the already-validated DbFingerprint.active_edge_count in try_load_snapshot and reject (fall back to load_from_db) on mismatch, turning the count fingerprint into an explicit bound rather than an implicit one.

