**Condvar blocking behavior (pool exhaustion) is never tested with actual threads**

- **ID:** `pool/testing-concurrent-read-exhaustion-no-multithreaded-test`
- **Severity:** medium
- **Area:** core · **Type:** test
- **Location:** `src/pool/connection_pool.rs:187-202`
- **Auto-fixable:** no

## Description
The test pool_file_backed_concurrent_reads (line 268) opens a pool with read_pool_size=2 and checks out 2 connections simultaneously — but all on the same thread. The Condvar/blocking path (line 195-196: `while conns.is_empty() { self.read_available.wait(&mut conns); }`) is exercised only trivially. There is no test that spawns N+1 threads to actually block one reader until a guard is dropped, proving the Condvar wakeup fires correctly. A deadlock or spurious-wakeup bug in the notify_one path would not be caught by existing tests.

## Suggested approach
Add a multithreaded exhaustion test:
```rust
#[test]
fn pool_read_blocks_and_unblocks_when_returned() {
    use std::sync::Arc;
    use std::thread;

    let dir = tempfile::tempdir().unwrap();
    let db_path = dir.path().join("test.db");
    // Pool with exactly 1 read connection
    let pool = Arc::new(ConnectionPool::open(&db_path, 4, 1, None).unwrap());

    let pool2 = Arc::clone(&pool);
    let r1 = pool.read(); // exhausts the pool

    let handle = thread::spawn(move || {
        // This will block until r1 is dropped
        let _r2 = pool2.read();
    });

    drop(r1); // triggers notify_one
    handle.join().unwrap(); // second read must succeed
}
```

